The man is running in the same direction as the train, so his speed reduces the effective length of the train that has to pass him.  
Effective length = \(220 - 8 = 212\) m.  
Relative speed = \(80 - 8 = 72\) km/h.  

Using the crossing-time formula:  
\[
\text{time}=\frac{\text{effective length} \times 18}{\text{relative speed}}
=\frac{212 \times 18}{72}
=\frac{212}{4}
=53
\]

